The diagram is drawn by the candidate. A full-mark diagram is large, drawn with a sharp pencil, with ruled label lines that do not cross and a title (*Motor neurone*). It shows, from left to right:

- the **cell body** (cyton) with a large **nucleus**, containing **Nissl granules** (ribosomes) in the cytoplasm, lying in the grey matter of the spinal cord;
- several short branched **dendrites** (dendrons) leaving the cell body;
- a long **axon** (nerve fibre) leaving the cell body, surrounded by a **myelin sheath** made by the **Schwann cells**;
- gaps in the myelin sheath, the **nodes of Ranvier**;
- the **axon terminals** (branched nerve endings) ending on a muscle fibre or gland (the **motor end plate**);
- an **arrow** showing that the nerve impulse travels from the cell body towards the effector.

Marking: correct shape and size of the drawing, and at least six correct labels.
